## Prospective work and cost

The exact 132-window schedule has 3 warm and 8 measured rounds for each fixture
and arm, rotating order. Each window constructs one factor and executes 16
FTRAN/FTRAN/BTRAN triplets. Including lifecycles: 141 fresh LU, 9 lu! attempts
(3 expected singular), 3 shallow copies, 4245 FTRAN, 2133 BTRAN and 12 snapshots.
These are entry counts, not complete native operation counts.

Constructor timing includes fresh control creation, its factor-owned copy,
native setup and scratch. Solve timing includes dimension/alias checks, counters,
and four scalar Info stores per call. This equal diagnostic overhead is disclosed,
not subtracted. All 6336 window destinations are allocated before measurement:
405504 Float64 entries, 3244032 bytes of payload, plus headers. The 132 preallocated
4-by-48 Info arrays add 25344 Float64 entries, 202752 bytes. Lifecycle destinations
add 42 length32 vectors (1344 elements); three 4-by-14 Info arrays add 168 elements.
Their roots are scoped before cost windows; scope exit is not native-free proof.

Outside timing, ownership and control checks scan CSC arrays and control vectors;
hex serialization copies outputs and metadata to strings. Twelve final snapshots
retain up to 2*n*(n+1)/2 factor entries each, plus indices, permutations and scales.
They allocate bounded arrays and compute a reachable Julia graph. Native Info
NUMERIC_SIZE/PEAK_MEMORY use SIZE_OF_UNIT and are not a native allocation/free
ledger. R additionally owns 4n Float64 scratch elements (32n bytes) per workspace,
including the copied workspace. No unnecessary NEW production work or retained
memory is introduced: production is unchanged. Diagnostic overhead affects the
45-second budget, cache/GC and timing context; this is not an end-to-end benchmark.

